{"reason": "The execution results show clear overfitting: training loss continues decreasing while validation log-loss plateaus at ~0.372 after epoch 4, then degrades. This aligns with the initial plan's analysis. The similar historical experiment successfully used a single linear head with cosine annealing warm restarts and achieved good results, but here the learning rate schedule is conflicted (manual warmup fights CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts), causing the LR to get stuck at 2e-5 after warmup and never decay. The model design needs enhancement to prevent overfitting and better utilize multi-layer representations. Therefore, I choose to modify both 'model_design' (add multi-sample dropout and mean pooling) and 'training_evaluation' (fix scheduler, add augmentations) as proposed in the initial plan. The data processing module is kept unchanged because the current feature engineering is already comprehensive and not the bottleneck.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace the simple linear head with multi-sample dropout (3 parallel paths with dropout rates 0.2, 0.3, 0.4) and mean pooling over the last 4 hidden layers instead of CLS token from last layer. Add a projection layer (Linear -> GELU -> LayerNorm -> Dropout -> Linear) before classification to increase capacity without unfreezing more backbone layers. This modification directly addresses overfitting by providing richer, regularized representations from multiple hidden layers, as similar historical experiments showed mean pooling improves generalization on small datasets.", "training_evaluation": "Replace the current conflicted scheduler (manual warmup inside CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts) with a proper get_linear_schedule_with_warmup: 10% warmup steps, then linear decay to 0. Increase max_lr to 3e-5 for backbone and 6e-5 for head. Add on-the-fly text augmentations in the dataset: synonym replacement (20% of words) and random word dropout (5% of words) with 50% probability. These changes ensure stable learning rate progression to avoid plateau, and augmentations combat overfitting by forcing the model to learn stylistic patterns instead of memorizing specific words, as validated in similar experiments."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The execution results show clear overfitting: training loss continues decreasing while validation log-loss plateaus at ~0.372 after epoch 4, then degrades. This aligns with the initial plan's analysis. The similar historical experiment successfully used a single linear head with cosine annealing warm restarts and achieved good results, but here the learning rate schedule is conflicted (manual warmup fights CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts), causing the LR to get stuck at 2e-5 after warmup and never decay. The model design needs enhancement to prevent overfitting and better utilize multi-layer representations. Therefore, I choose to modify both 'model_design' (add multi-sample dropout and mean pooling) and 'training_evaluation' (fix scheduler, add augmentations) as proposed in the initial plan. The data processing module is kept unchanged because the current feature engineering is already comprehensive and not the bottleneck.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace the simple linear head with multi-sample dropout (3 parallel paths with dropout rates 0.2, 0.3, 0.4) and mean pooling over the last 4 hidden layers instead of CLS token from last layer. Add a projection layer (Linear -> GELU -> LayerNorm -> Dropout -> Linear) before classification to increase capacity without unfreezing more backbone layers. This modification directly addresses overfitting by providing richer, regularized representations from multiple hidden layers, as similar historical experiments showed mean pooling improves generalization on small datasets.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the current conflicted scheduler (manual warmup inside CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts) with a proper get_linear_schedule_with_warmup: 10% warmup steps, then linear decay to 0. Increase max_lr to 3e-5 for backbone and 6e-5 for head. Add on-the-fly text augmentations in the dataset: synonym replacement (20% of words) and random word dropout (5% of words) with 50% probability. These changes ensure stable learning rate progression to avoid plateau, and augmentations combat overfitting by forcing the model to learn stylistic patterns instead of memorizing specific words, as validated in similar experiments."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The current validation log-loss reaches 0.1640 but oscillates (e.g., 0.1667→0.1760→0.1713→0.1645→0.1693), indicating instability from single dropout sampling. The initial plan proposes multi-sample dropout (K=4) and an auxiliary task for sentence-start features to reduce variance and exploit author-specific stylistic cues. Historical experiments show that simpler schedulers (linear warmup with cosine decay) and DeBERTa-v3-base are stable, while complex schedulers can cause NaN. The refinement selects 'model_design' to implement the multi-sample dropout and auxiliary head, and 'training_evaluation' to modify inference to average multiple passes. Data processing is kept unchanged as feature engineering is already integrated via the auxiliary task.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Modify the SpookyAuthorClassifier forward method to return both main logits and auxiliary features for multi-sample dropout with K=4. Specifically, in forward, after obtaining the CLS vector from the backbone, apply dropout K times to the CLS vector (using a loop or stacked operations), pass each through the dropout and linear head, and store the logits. Compute the mean of the K logits as the main output. Additionally, add a small 2-layer MLP (64 hidden, ReLU) on top of the CLS vector (before dropout) to predict a 50-dimensional binary vector representing the presence of top-50 starting words/bigrams. The auxiliary head is trained with binary cross-entropy loss (summed with main CE via weighting lambda=0.5). During evaluation, use the mean of K forward passes (same dropout masks) for final logits. This addresses the variance issue by averaging and enforces structural feature learning. Keep all other model parameters (backbone freezing, optimizer, etc.) unchanged.", "training_evaluation": "Modify the training loop to compute auxiliary head loss: after forward, extract the auxiliary predictions from the model (the model returns main_logits, aux_logits, and aux_labels placeholder). Compute BCE loss between aux_logits and aux_targets (binary vectors derived from sentence texts using precomputed top-50 word set). Use total_loss = CE_main + 0.5 * BCE_aux. For the test inference, perform K=4 forward passes with different dropout masks (enabling dropout in eval mode) and average the logits before softmax. Ensure no NaN by adding tiny epsilon. Adjust validation loop similarly to average K passes. Keep all other training settings (batch size, epochs, early stopping, etc.) as is."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The current validation log-loss reaches 0.1640 but oscillates (e.g., 0.1667→0.1760→0.1713→0.1645→0.1693), indicating instability from single dropout sampling. The initial plan proposes multi-sample dropout (K=4) and an auxiliary task for sentence-start features to reduce variance and exploit author-specific stylistic cues. Historical experiments show that simpler schedulers (linear warmup with cosine decay) and DeBERTa-v3-base are stable, while complex schedulers can cause NaN. The refinement selects 'model_design' to implement the multi-sample dropout and auxiliary head, and 'training_evaluation' to modify inference to average multiple passes. Data processing is kept unchanged as feature engineering is already integrated via the auxiliary task.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Modify the SpookyAuthorClassifier forward method to return both main logits and auxiliary features for multi-sample dropout with K=4. Specifically, in forward, after obtaining the CLS vector from the backbone, apply dropout K times to the CLS vector (using a loop or stacked operations), pass each through the dropout and linear head, and store the logits. Compute the mean of the K logits as the main output. Additionally, add a small 2-layer MLP (64 hidden, ReLU) on top of the CLS vector (before dropout) to predict a 50-dimensional binary vector representing the presence of top-50 starting words/bigrams. The auxiliary head is trained with binary cross-entropy loss (summed with main CE via weighting lambda=0.5). During evaluation, use the mean of K forward passes (same dropout masks) for final logits. This addresses the variance issue by averaging and enforces structural feature learning. Keep all other model parameters (backbone freezing, optimizer, etc.) unchanged.",
    "training_evaluation": "Modify the training loop to compute auxiliary head loss: after forward, extract the auxiliary predictions from the model (the model returns main_logits, aux_logits, and aux_labels placeholder). Compute BCE loss between aux_logits and aux_targets (binary vectors derived from sentence texts using precomputed top-50 word set). Use total_loss = CE_main + 0.5 * BCE_aux. For the test inference, perform K=4 forward passes with different dropout masks (enabling dropout in eval mode) and average the logits before softmax. Ensure no NaN by adding tiny epsilon. Adjust validation loop similarly to average K passes. Keep all other training settings (batch size, epochs, early stopping, etc.) as is."
  }
}"}

{"reason": "The validation log-loss reached 0.1901 but plateaued around 0.19, indicating mild overfitting and limited representation learning from the weak auxiliary task (sentence-start bigrams). Historical experiments show that contrastive learning on [CLS] embeddings (instead of auxiliary task) plus OneCycleLR and stronger regularization improved performance significantly. The changes focus on model_design (replace auxiliary head with contrastive learning, increase dropout, add stochastic depth) and training_evaluation (switch scheduler to OneCycleLR, adjust loss weighting). Data processing changes are not needed as the current approach is sufficient.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Remove the auxiliary head (predicting starting-word bigrams) and replace it with a supervised contrastive learning objective on [CLS] embeddings. Specifically, in forward, after obtaining the CLS vector, apply a projection MLP (256-dim hidden, ReLU, output 128-dim embedding). During training, compute contrastive loss (NT-Xent) where positives are same-author samples in the batch. Keep the classification head unchanged. Increase hidden dropout from 0.3 to 0.4 and add stochastic depth with drop rate 0.1 (drop entire layers in DeBERTa encoder probabilistically). This addresses the weak auxiliary signal and overfitting, directly learning author-discriminative embeddings.", "training_evaluation": "Switch the optimizer scheduler from cosine warmup to AdamW with OneCycleLR (max_lr=2e-5, pct_start=0.1, final_div_factor=100). Remove the auxiliary loss and set total loss = classification CE + 0.2 * contrastive loss. Keep batch size 16, max length 512, epochs 30, label smoothing 0.1, multi-sample dropout (num_samples=4) during inference. The OneCycleLR stabilizes combined gradient signals and helps convergence."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The validation log-loss reached 0.1901 but plateaued around 0.19, indicating mild overfitting and limited representation learning from the weak auxiliary task (sentence-start bigrams). Historical experiments show that contrastive learning on [CLS] embeddings (instead of auxiliary task) plus OneCycleLR and stronger regularization improved performance significantly. The changes focus on model_design (replace auxiliary head with contrastive learning, increase dropout, add stochastic depth) and training_evaluation (switch scheduler to OneCycleLR, adjust loss weighting). Data processing changes are not needed as the current approach is sufficient.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
