{"reason": "The initial plan's root causes are suboptimal pooling and training instability, both reflected in the execution results. The validation log-loss plateaued at 0.1235 with high variance, indicating underfitting due to [CLS] token pooling and a complex per-batch scheduler. Historical experiments confirm that multi-pooling (MHA + mean-max) significantly improves representation for long texts, and OneCycleLR provides stable convergence. Model design is the primary module for pooling enhancement, training evaluation for scheduler overhaul. Data processing remains unchanged as no feature engineering is needed.", "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"model_design": "Replace [CLS] token pooling with a Multi-Head Attention (MHA) + Mean-Max Concatenation pooling module. Implement a learned attention head (hidden_size, num_heads=8) over token hidden states, compute mean and max pooling across sequence length, concatenate all three representations (3 * hidden_size), apply a linear projection to hidden_size with dropout=0.3, then pass to the classification head. This change directly addresses the need to capture authorial style distributed across the entire text, as validated by similar experiments where multi-pooling improved validation log-loss by ~0.02. The backbone (last 8 unfrozen layers) and head structure remain intact; only the pooling mechanism in forward() is modified.", "training_evaluation": "Replace the current per-batch linear warmup + CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler with a per-epoch OneCycleLR scheduler. Set max_lr=2e-5 for backbone (last 8 unfrozen layers) and 5e-5 for head, pct_start=0.1, final_div_factor=1000, and perform step per epoch. Remove manual warmup logic and per-batch scheduler updates. This simplifies training, reduces variance, and ensures stable convergence, as observed in historical experiments where OneCycleLR led to faster convergence and better final log-loss (from 0.1235 to ~0.10). All other training components (loss, optimizer, batch size, early stopping, mixed precision) remain unchanged."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
  "reason": "The initial plan's root causes are suboptimal pooling and training instability, both reflected in the execution results. The validation log-loss plateaued at 0.1235 with high variance, indicating underfitting due to [CLS] token pooling and a complex per-batch scheduler. Historical experiments confirm that multi-pooling (MHA + mean-max) significantly improves representation for long texts, and OneCycleLR provides stable convergence. Model design is the primary module for pooling enhancement, training evaluation for scheduler overhaul. Data processing remains unchanged as no feature engineering is needed.",
  "module": ["model_design", "training_evaluation"],
  "plan": {
    "model_design": "Replace [CLS] token pooling with a Multi-Head Attention (MHA) + Mean-Max Concatenation pooling module. Implement a learned attention head (hidden_size, num_heads=8) over token hidden states, compute mean and max pooling across sequence length, concatenate all three representations (3 * hidden_size), apply a linear projection to hidden_size with dropout=0.3, then pass to the classification head. This change directly addresses the need to capture authorial style distributed across the entire text, as validated by similar experiments where multi-pooling improved validation log-loss by ~0.02. The backbone (last 8 unfrozen layers) and head structure remain intact; only the pooling mechanism in forward() is modified.",
    "training_evaluation": "Replace the current per-batch linear warmup + CosineAnnealingWarmRestarts scheduler with a per-epoch OneCycleLR scheduler. Set max_lr=2e-5 for backbone (last 8 unfrozen layers) and 5e-5 for head, pct_start=0.1, final_div_factor=1000, and perform step per epoch. Remove manual warmup logic and per-batch scheduler updates. This simplifies training, reduces variance, and ensures stable convergence, as observed in historical experiments where OneCycleLR led to faster convergence and better final log-loss (from 0.1235 to ~0.10). All other training components (loss, optimizer, batch size, early stopping, mixed precision) remain unchanged."
