{"reason": "The initial plan aims to break a log-loss plateau by ensembling diverse architectures (DeBERTa-v3-base, RoBERTa-base, DeBERTa-v3-small) with engineered features and a meta-learner, addressing single-model bias. Current results show plateauing around 0.1640 with oscillations, confirming the need. Historical similar experiments show that simpler scheduling (linear warmup + cosine decay) and stable DeBERTa-v3-base are reliable, while complex schedulers and architectures cause NaN or instability. Refinement selects only 'training_evaluation' because the code already has a DeBERTa-v3-base backbone that can be retained as one ensemble component; we should not add new models or change architecture yet (to avoid risk), but instead focus on implementing the meta-learner blending with out-of-fold predictions from the existing model across multiple seeds/folds. Additionally, engineered features (sentence length, punctuation) can be added via data processing to improve diversity. Historical successes show that ensembling with simple averaging or linear blending across different seeds/folds yields improvement without architectural complexity. Thus, 'data_processing_and_feature_engineering' and 'training_evaluation' are selected.", "module": ["data_processing_and_feature_engineering", "training_evaluation"], "plan": {"data_processing_and_feature_engineering": "Add engineered features: sentence length (number of characters), punctuation density (count of .!? / length), and word count. Compute these from training set texts, fit a StandardScaler on the training set only, then transform for train, validation, and test. Append these 3 features to the tokenizer output as additional input tensors (e.g., 'extra_features') in the dataset __getitem__. This injects stylistic cues that are strong discriminators (Poe uses dense punctuation and short sentences, Lovecraft uses long rolling sentences), as informed by the initial plan. Preserve all existing variable names (train_texts_final, etc.) and dataset class interface.", "training_evaluation": "Modify the training and evaluation loop to support 5-fold cross-validation with different random seeds (42, 123, 456) for the existing DeBERTa-v3-base model. For each seed, perform 5-fold training, collect out-of-fold predictions on validation folds, and save each fold's model. After all seeds, aggregate out-of-fold predictions across all folds and seeds (15 models total). Train a softmax-weighted linear blending model on these out-of-fold predictions using log-loss as objective (e.g., using scipy.optimize.minimize). For test inference, load all 15 models, predict on test data, and apply the learned blend weights to produce final probabilities. This ensemble approach reduces variance and leverages complementary information from different training dynamics, directly addressing the plateau as per the initial plan. Keep all other training hyperparameters (batch size, learning rate, scheduler, etc.) unchanged from the current code. Use the same label smoothing and gradient clipping to avoid NaN issues noted in similar failed experiments."}, "parse_success": true, "raw_response": "{
